WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:10.459
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: May we then move on and turn
to the next item on the agenda, which is Taking

00:00:10.459 --> 00:00:14.919
the Cancer Strategy Forward, the Programme
Update. Cally, you are very welcome, very

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:19.680
pleased to have you with us, this morning.
I am going to invite Bruce to introduce the

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:24.280
paper, but then we are interested to hear
from both of you.

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:30.359
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: The aim of this paper is to
give you an update on where we are with the

00:00:30.359 --> 00:00:36.630
Cancer Strategy, which was published in July
2015, and focused largely on how to prevent

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:44.431
cancer, how to diagnose it early when it occurs,
how to upgrade our treatments and how to ensure

00:00:44.431 --> 00:00:50.020
that people have as good an experience as
they possibly can under difficult circumstances.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:56.500
We are now in the implementation phase of
that strategy, but it is worth saying, to

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:02.910
support the Chief Executive’s remarks, that
survival rates have never been higher in cancer,

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:08.600
but the aim of the Cancer Strategy is to act
as an accelerant to improve survival rates,

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:12.340
and, indeed, to prevent cancer where at all
possible.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:18.340
What I would like to say before Cally gives
us an update is: there has been a bit of a

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:22.310
discussion recently about the availability
of cancer drugs, and I thought I might just

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:29.280
take the opportunity to update the Board on
one or two issues. Cancer drugs are getting

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:36.890
both better at treating cancer and more expensive,
and some of those only work on a limited proportion

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:42.060
of people with a given type of cancer. You
will recall that we have transitioned from

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:47.869
an old-style Cancer Drugs Fund to a new-style
Cancer Drugs Fund, which aims to offer faster

00:01:47.869 --> 00:01:57.659
access to newer treatments and better value
for money for the taxpayer. In addition, all

00:01:57.659 --> 00:02:03.369
new cancer drugs will be assessed through
the Cancer Drugs Fund and, in order to speed

00:02:03.369 --> 00:02:10.530
things up, NICE has put in place mechanisms
for ensuring that recommendations on whether

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:17.480
a drug should be approved or not will occur
at or before the time of market authorisation,

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:22.750
so that will speed up access of new drugs
with a positive recommendation for those people

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:27.860
with cancer. When that happens, that means
that immediate funding will become available

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:34.989
and be put into the commissioning baseline.
If the evidence which NICE is assessing leaves

00:02:34.989 --> 00:02:39.329
some level of uncertainty about the efficacy
or cost-effectiveness of a drug, it will go

00:02:39.329 --> 00:02:45.190
into the new Cancer Drugs Fund for a period
of assessment but will continue to be funded

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:49.480
while it is in the Cancer Drugs Fund.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:55.630
The transition from the old to the new has
been quite successful; 41 of 43 treatments

00:02:55.630 --> 00:03:01.900
in the old Cancer Drugs Fund transitioned
to the new. Of those that have been reassessed,

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:08.760
seven out of nine have been successful. I
think we owe some thanks to the pharmaceutical

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:14.780
industry both for providing new evidence where
that has been helpful, and for then reviewing

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:24.430
their prices, which alters the equation in
favour of approval. In addition, since 1st

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:30.730
April this year, 40 new cancer drugs have
been approved, including one new highly effective

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:36.630
cancer drug for lung cancer. That is just
a kind of thumbnail sketch to bring you up

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:41.290
to speed with where we are on the Cancer Drugs
Fund, and now perhaps I could hand over to

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:47.530
Cally, who you all know is Chief Executive
of the world-class Royal Marsden Hospital,

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:53.710
and is our National Cancer Director, to bring
us up to speed on progress over the last year.

00:03:55.320 --> 00:04:00.180
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: As Bruce has said, the Task
Force aim is covering everything from upgrading,

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:04.790
prevention, through to early diagnosis, and
improved treatment and care. Equal emphasis

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:08.870
on survival and quality of life, and patient
experience, so that is quite important; those

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:14.080
are on a par. Both survival and experience
are at the centre of the strategy and the

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:18.970
implementation plan. Very importantly, as
I mentioned last time I addressed the Board,

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:24.030
this is also about dealing with variation
in access to care and the standard of delivery

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:28.790
of care across the country, so that this business
of unwanted variation we need to deal with

00:04:28.790 --> 00:04:31.170
as part of the strategy delivery.

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:35.412
I will just make a couple of remarks about
the principles behind some of the work that

00:04:35.412 --> 00:04:38.600
I have doing, then I will select some examples
not from the Cancer Drugs Fund, but the other

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:45.440
examples from the paper that came to the Board.
The principles that I am working to are, first

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:50.220
of all, integration; and this is integrating
care across whole pathways, because for patients,

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:54.280
they bounce around in the system too much,
so we need to get patients in in a speedy

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:59.360
manner, and then through to definitive diagnosis
and treatment quickly. There are areas of

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:03.090
fantastic practice throughout the country,
but it is this integration of care across

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:07.190
whole pathways that is going to be important,
and I will come back to some of the mechanisms

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:08.290
behind that.

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:15.560
Secondly, there is an issue about information,
both to patients and clinical professionals,

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:19.750
and it is providing information across those
pathways, so that we avoid these – I think

00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:23.580
Simon talked about ‘silos’, or the Chairman
talked about silos; but it is making sure

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:28.000
that we look at the whole performance across
pathways for whole populations in trying to

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.280
deal with any deficits or gaps.

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:36.120
Integrated care, better information across
pathways, and then targeting resources, whether

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:42.580
you are talking about genomics, or drugs,
or modern radiotherapy treatment. Modern cancer

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:47.140
treatment is all about precision, so precision
radiotherapy, to spare healthy tissues and

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:52.890
really target the cancer, and improve survival
effectively; precision drug treatment with

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:58.020
a molecular basis behind it; and then precision
surgery, so minimal access surgery. It is

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:06.020
all about precision, and really treating patients
with the best technology, short lengths of

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:08.780
stay throughout the country.

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:15.410
We have just published a document called One
Year On in October 2016, just explaining what

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:20.370
the Task Force ambitions were, the activity
that we have undertaken over the last year,

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:24.130
and some of the evaluation and the metrics
that we will use to assess whether we have

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:29.621
been successful or not. I will just select
a few of those, missing genomics and cancer

00:06:29.621 --> 00:06:33.950
drugs, because the Chairman and Bruce have
touched on those. The first thing to let you

00:06:33.950 --> 00:06:40.270
know is that we have established 16 cancer
alliances around England, which broadly cover

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:44.600
two or three STP areas, so we are not trying
to create extra footprints and extra complexity;

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:49.250
we are trying to say, ‘Here is the focus
for cancer for local delivery of improvement

00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:52.800
through a cancer alliance, which will bring
together clinical teams across those pathways

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:57.562
for specific populations.’ They are not
the old cancer networks, but they are a modern

00:06:57.562 --> 00:07:02.260
version of that to deliver the national strategy.
The cancer alliance footprints, as you will

00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:07.010
have seen from the paper, have been identified
and established. We have held our first event

00:07:07.010 --> 00:07:11.860
for alliance leads, so senior clinicians and
chief executive leads. I have gone around

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:15.530
the country to talk to people about the purpose
of the alliances, the levers, the incentives,

00:07:15.530 --> 00:07:18.530
the way in which they will work, and I have
been really pleased that there has been a

00:07:18.530 --> 00:07:24.580
lot of support for them. We are getting going,
and the key thing to do in the next two or

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:29.490
three months at the outside is to make sure
that each of those alliances has a delivery

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:35.800
plan, which will tackle things like pathway
improvement, 62-day waits, any workforce issues,

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:40.160
so that whatever plan we have at the centre,
there is a local delivery system, so building

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:43.860
the identity, the leadership teams of the
alliances will be very important over the

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:45.540
next few months.

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:52.730
Then we have three cancer vanguard partnerships
which, if you like, are alliances plus-plus.

00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:58.889
These are going to be experimenting in Manchester
and two partnerships in London, with a completely

00:07:58.889 --> 00:08:03.740
different model of commissioning and provision
for cancer. It is loosely based on the ACO

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:08.570
model in the States, but translated into a
UK context where, by giving the senior clinical

00:08:08.570 --> 00:08:14.430
teams responsibility for provisioning commissioning
for a whole population for a whole pathway

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:21.402
for cancer, they can drive some improvements
faster. So, the 16 cancer alliances and the

00:08:21.402 --> 00:08:25.720
three cancer vanguards are established to
create that level of change and accelerate

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:30.170
improvement for patients throughout the country.

00:08:30.170 --> 00:08:35.990
The second suite of measures has really been
around early diagnosis, so if you look at

00:08:35.990 --> 00:08:41.760
some of the CCG performance criteria, you
look at patient experience, you look at the

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:46.870
late stage of presentation for many patients,
we really have to improve the stage at which

00:08:46.870 --> 00:08:52.730
patients are diagnosed, so that we see many
more patients being diagnosed at stage I and

00:08:52.730 --> 00:08:58.089
stage II of their cancers. There is a suite
of measures about early diagnosis: the first

00:08:58.089 --> 00:09:03.700
is to create a new 28-day faster diagnosis
standard, so we can measure not just speed

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:09.410
to definitive treatment, but speed to definitive
diagnosis or exclusion of a diagnosis of cancer.

00:09:09.410 --> 00:09:13.350
We are working on that with different regions
at the moment, to just work out how we do

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:17.430
that effectively, so for a patient you know,
‘Right, I will know whether I have cancer

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:21.450
or not within 28 days, and then within the
whole 62-day pathway, I will get my first

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:25.689
definitive treatment, if that is what I need.’

00:09:25.689 --> 00:09:31.010
Apart from the standard, we have established
a diagnostic capacity fund, and the point

00:09:31.010 --> 00:09:35.870
of that over the next four years is to drive
different models of rapid diagnosis and assessment

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:43.070
for patients throughout the alliances. I like
to say it is a mixture of heavy lifting of

00:09:43.070 --> 00:09:47.810
changing care models, but also some investment,
to really get an injection behind changing

00:09:47.810 --> 00:09:53.940
some of those diagnostic pathways and improving
them. So, the fund, the looking at the new

00:09:53.940 --> 00:10:00.640
models and the 28-day standard, and then measuring
speed into the system, will hopefully create

00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:04.020
the kind of improvements we want to see, both
in patient experience and, importantly, patient

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:04.640
survival.

00:10:07.670 --> 00:10:12.540
The next area to cover is the modernisation
of radiotherapy. I mentioned that cancer medicine

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:19.330
is all about precision. Radiotherapy is quite
straightforward: we need the latest intensity-modulated

00:10:19.330 --> 00:10:24.400
radiotherapy to be available throughout the
country to all patients. So, as you will have

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:28.430
seen from the paper, I am absolutely delighted
that there has been a £130&nbsp;million investment

00:10:28.430 --> 00:10:33.380
in radiotherapy, and that will take care in
the next two years of over half the modernisation

00:10:33.380 --> 00:10:39.210
requirements the Task Force set for us. Alongside
the modernisation of technology, so all patients

00:10:39.210 --> 00:10:44.380
have access to the latest and best radiotherapy
treatment, we will be creating some radiotherapy

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:49.380
networks which will map onto the alliances,
so that the service model and the technology

00:10:49.380 --> 00:10:57.050
will all be aligned. That is really fantastic
news for patients, I think.

00:10:57.050 --> 00:11:01.750
The last couple of things to touch on; so,
I have mentioned the importance of experience

00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:06.990
and quality for patients, as well as survival.
We are developing a quality-of-life indicator

00:11:06.990 --> 00:11:11.230
for the first time in the NHS, which is quite
a challenge because it is not done well anywhere

00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:17.960
internationally. It is to develop with patients
a quality-of-life indicator. So, many patients

00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:24.399
will survive for longer with a cancer diagnosis,
and we need to make sure that the impact of

00:11:24.399 --> 00:11:28.940
their treatment and the impact of their cancer,
we can support them with that effectively

00:11:28.940 --> 00:11:33.770
and measure the quality of life that they
have post-acute treatment. This will be ready

00:11:33.770 --> 00:11:37.470
by 2020 at the latest. I will try and get
it ready earlier if we can, but it is a really

00:11:37.470 --> 00:11:41.970
interesting way to try and measure something
beyond the measurement of speed to diagnosis

00:11:41.970 --> 00:11:46.830
and treatment, so to really appreciate what
quality-of-life impact someone might have,

00:11:46.830 --> 00:11:54.779
and how we can support them better through
the NHS with that. We have fantastic patient

00:11:54.779 --> 00:11:59.600
experience results nationally, but we can
always do better, and it is about supporting

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:05.120
people living with and beyond cancer, rather
than just the acute stage of treatment, now.

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:08.550
The final thing I will just touch on – it
is not a complete run-down of everything in

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:15.220
the paper – is: with an investment in the
cancer programme goes responsibility for improving

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:21.410
performance, and we are just creating an intensive
support team to tackle the 62-day pathway

00:12:21.410 --> 00:12:26.660
so that we can quickly see where the deficits
are in that whole pathway for patients, speed

00:12:26.660 --> 00:12:33.520
it up and improve it. We hover around 82–83%
at the moment; we need to get to 85%, and

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:38.450
we need to make sure that is a consistent
standard of performance. With the alliances,

00:12:38.450 --> 00:12:42.899
the delivery plans and the investment we are
making in diagnostic models, I hope it will

00:12:42.899 --> 00:12:48.709
be possible to change and successfully improve
that 62-day performance across the country.

00:12:53.780 --> 00:12:59.880
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: That was absolutely crystal
clear; I am very grateful for the presentation.

00:13:03.680 --> 00:13:06.920
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: First of all, Cally, to you
and your team, thank you. There has been a

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:16.910
huge amount of progress and, like you, I particularly
share the investment that is being made, which

00:13:16.910 --> 00:13:20.610
will make a real tangible difference. I have
personal experience; not myself, but in my

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:26.320
family. Indeed, one of the places I know is
receiving it, so it is great.

00:13:26.330 --> 00:13:32.250
I think we are focusing a lot of our effort
around STPs. Therefore, I would be interested

00:13:32.250 --> 00:13:40.740
to know two things, really. One, the extent
to which you are finding it helpful and easy

00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:50.380
to use those as vehicles to help create local
ownership and local speed of implementation.

00:13:50.380 --> 00:13:54.750
Then the second thing is, if there was one
thing or two things we could do to make your

00:13:54.750 --> 00:14:01.620
life easier, to get more rapid progress that
will make a difference for patients and their

00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:06.399
families, what would that be?
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: The first thing on the STPs

00:14:06.399 --> 00:14:12.820
is that I like to think about the cancer alliances
as the cancer work stream of the STPs. So,

00:14:12.820 --> 00:14:21.000
we have 16 alliances and three vanguards,
and obviously 44 STP footprints, but we have

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:24.890
tried to guess this coterminous approach so
that it is coherent for people within the

00:14:24.890 --> 00:14:32.850
system. The STPs are dealing with some very
big issues about pathways, deficits, ways

00:14:32.850 --> 00:14:38.670
to reconfigure to improve sustainability and
performance long term, so I think it is very

00:14:38.670 --> 00:14:43.390
helpful to have the cancer alliance teams
working alongside the STPs as the cancer work

00:14:43.390 --> 00:14:48.810
stream, so that is the intention.
I think we have to carry on that kind of working

00:14:48.810 --> 00:14:53.149
arrangement, but as I said earlier, the alliances
have been very well received by people working

00:14:53.149 --> 00:14:59.060
with STP footprints, and I think people think
it is sensible to view the alliances as the

00:14:59.060 --> 00:15:02.700
cancer work stream, to accelerate some of
this work that we need to deliver in the next

00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:08.850
four years. I think the alignment with the
STPs is good; we just need to carry on aligning

00:15:08.850 --> 00:15:13.190
and refining that, and particularly the delivery
plan around cancer.

00:15:13.190 --> 00:15:21.709
In terms of support, I think it is fantastic
that we have the investment, both in the Linacs

00:15:21.709 --> 00:15:27.040
and in the four years of the programme, because
it is really important for people when they

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:30.960
are changing diagnostic models and thinking
about pathways that it is not a 12-month or

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:35.100
a 24‑month visibility; that they can see
over a four-year plan, ‘This is what we

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:41.010
need to do for early diagnostics, for Yorkshire
or Devon, or wherever.’ Fantastic to have

00:15:41.010 --> 00:15:46.870
that level of investment.
I think what I would ask you to understand,

00:15:46.870 --> 00:15:52.870
really, or advise on, is that this is a really
big programme of work, and what I am trying

00:15:52.870 --> 00:15:56.829
to do is focus on the things that I think
will have the most impact on patients fastest.

00:15:56.829 --> 00:16:01.120
So, you will not see, for example, a lot of
information about – we have not made a lot

00:16:01.120 --> 00:16:05.769
of progress on the digital part of the strategy
yet. We will, we will get there, but I am

00:16:05.769 --> 00:16:09.950
really focusing on three or four things that
I think will make a massive difference to

00:16:09.950 --> 00:16:15.800
patients quickly. It is really: thank you
for the investment, thank you for the support,

00:16:15.800 --> 00:16:20.250
and I ask for your understanding that we are
going to really focus on things in a sequential

00:16:20.250 --> 00:16:22.250
way that we think will deliver results.
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: I do not think you need to

00:16:22.250 --> 00:16:26.410
ask for our understanding; I think it is music
to our ears that you are choosing to focus

00:16:26.410 --> 00:16:30.880
on three or four things and get them done.
There are always three answers to strategy

00:16:30.880 --> 00:16:36.740
questions: yes, no and not now, and the not
now is the one that is often ignored. So actually,

00:16:36.740 --> 00:16:43.260
I think you will find us your strongest cheerleaders
for focusing on priorities.

00:16:43.260 --> 00:16:49.091
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: Great, fantastic. Thank you.
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: It is great news, Cally, that

00:16:49.091 --> 00:16:54.920
the national dials are starting to point in
the right direction. In cancer, as within

00:16:54.920 --> 00:17:01.560
many of the other national pathways, we are
inheriting a lot of variation.

00:17:01.560 --> 00:17:08.400
what's your sense about the impact that the three or four things - or indeed the many things -

00:17:08.409 --> 00:17:14.850
are having on the issue of variation as opposed
to just the national aggregate numbers?

00:17:16.660 --> 00:17:22.140
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: I think what will be critical
is the quality and nature of the delivery

00:17:22.149 --> 00:17:26.559
plans for the cancer alliances as they develop
them over the next two to three months, because

00:17:26.559 --> 00:17:30.489
that Is where we are asking people to say,
if these are the principles nationally, what

00:17:30.489 --> 00:17:35.669
are your deficits locally, and how does a
CCG to acute trust to specialist centre work?

00:17:35.669 --> 00:17:44.600
Where are the problems? We can see, if we
look at the CCG performance – so, we look

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:49.480
at the four cancer metrics that the CCGs are
being measured on – early diagnosis is the

00:17:49.480 --> 00:17:54.230
one that trips most of them up, where they
are not performing at the level we need. So,

00:17:54.230 --> 00:18:00.230
that is why I think this huge focus on diagnostic
models and speed in should make a big difference

00:18:00.230 --> 00:18:04.690
to variation in speed of patient access, and
therefore quality of survival and experience

00:18:04.690 --> 00:18:09.460
for people, and things like stopping too many
people being diagnosed as an emergency in

00:18:09.460 --> 00:18:15.999
A&amp;E, which we absolutely do not want. I think
delivery plans will be key to addressing variation,

00:18:15.999 --> 00:18:22.299
and I am quite confident that there is huge
support for those alliances, and now we have

00:18:22.299 --> 00:18:26.910
some transformation funding, we can create
some levers to change those models, but we

00:18:26.910 --> 00:18:31.899
have to get that early diagnosis piece right
to really deal properly with variation. It

00:18:31.899 --> 00:18:36.369
is great that we are doing the Linacs and
radiotherapy, that is one end, and we will

00:18:36.369 --> 00:18:40.559
get that sustainable and high quality throughout
England, but we really have to get that early

00:18:40.559 --> 00:18:45.590
front end of the pathway sorted, and then
I think we are really on a good path.

00:18:45.590 --> 00:18:52.070
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Just to say, the data that
NHS Digital published a fortnight ago on one-year

00:18:52.070 --> 00:18:56.799
cancer survival rates showed that the inequality
between parts of the country has been narrowing,

00:18:56.799 --> 00:19:04.440
so as well as the one-year survival going
up from 60% to 70% since 1999, the gap between

00:19:04.440 --> 00:19:10.200
individual CCGs has been converging, so those
places that were doing worst have been improving

00:19:10.200 --> 00:19:12.920
fastest.
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Can I just follow up on the

00:19:12.920 --> 00:19:16.340
variation, which I think is a key dimension
in all of this? Because you said at the outset

00:19:16.340 --> 00:19:21.450
that one of the big issues was both variable
access and variable quality of treatment.

00:19:21.450 --> 00:19:26.110
So, we have some of the world’s top cancer
hospitals, led by research, highly intensive,

00:19:26.110 --> 00:19:31.450
doing all the things that you just described
before, and so intuitively, one would expect

00:19:31.450 --> 00:19:35.919
that outcomes were better in some of our treatment
centres than elsewhere. I am just interested

00:19:35.919 --> 00:19:42.509
to understand, through the alliances, how
it is possible to tackle some of those variations?

00:19:42.509 --> 00:19:46.239
Is it spreading the practice of the best,
is it having a single medical director over

00:19:46.239 --> 00:19:52.249
a larger region driving quality throughout
the region, or is it still trying to develop

00:19:52.249 --> 00:19:58.009
a better performance amongst a fragmented
model of care?

00:20:00.060 --> 00:20:04.880
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: The first thing to say is
that with the cancer vanguards, which is the

00:20:04.890 --> 00:20:09.649
biggest sort of experiment with the commissioning
and provision model, having a lead provider

00:20:09.649 --> 00:20:13.889
– so UCLH and partners, the Christie in
Greater Manchester, the Royal Marsden and

00:20:13.889 --> 00:20:19.519
partners – having a lead provider model
is quite an important fundamental principle

00:20:19.519 --> 00:20:24.509
in seeing if you can drive that change through
that lead provider, and it is really important

00:20:24.509 --> 00:20:29.179
to look at that with a multi-speciality lead
provider like UCLH, as well as with the specialist

00:20:29.179 --> 00:20:33.519
cancer centres like the Christie and the Royal
Marsden. I think the lead provider model is

00:20:33.519 --> 00:20:38.480
interesting and important to see whether that
is something that can drive that whole pathway,

00:20:38.480 --> 00:20:47.739
whole population improvement. In my experience,
I have seen that if you give senior clinical

00:20:47.739 --> 00:20:53.820
teams pathway information, staging data, so
speed of presentation for patients; if you

00:20:53.820 --> 00:20:58.649
give it to them across the pathway, they start
to correct where there is less than good practice

00:20:58.649 --> 00:21:04.279
or best practice. It is giving them the information
across the pathway, some transformation funding

00:21:04.279 --> 00:21:09.299
to change the levers, and getting clinical
teams to function on that, so that is what

00:21:09.299 --> 00:21:15.200
I am expecting will drive the change. I have
seen it. I think if individual hospitals talk

00:21:15.200 --> 00:21:18.440
about their own trajectory and their performance,
that is one thing, but when you get clinical

00:21:18.440 --> 00:21:23.720
teams working across the pathway looking at,
‘Oh, here there are patients not getting

00:21:23.720 --> 00:21:28.420
in at stage I and II of their cancer, that
is too late, and that is very different from

00:21:28.420 --> 00:21:32.560
this provider,’ then it starts to drive
a sort of peer pressure as people start to

00:21:32.570 --> 00:21:37.200
say, ‘How can we improve the use of those
resources, speed into the system and staging

00:21:37.200 --> 00:21:40.580
data?’ We have some real evidence behind
that from some work that has been done across

00:21:40.580 --> 00:21:42.340
London.
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: So, it is the transparency

00:21:42.340 --> 00:21:43.769
which is driving change?
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: It is the transparency of

00:21:43.769 --> 00:21:47.789
the pathway, so the lead provider model, and
then generally for the alliances – because

00:21:47.789 --> 00:21:51.750
they are not into a lead provider model yet
– it is that transparency of information

00:21:51.750 --> 00:21:55.649
through the cancer dashboard of how they are
performing across the pathway, and getting

00:21:55.649 --> 00:22:00.139
GPs and oncologists to take a look at where
those deficits or gaps are.

00:22:00.139 --> 00:22:04.820
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Can I just ask a subsidiary
question, which is around workforce? One hears

00:22:04.820 --> 00:22:11.519
of workforce vacuums, for example, around
radiography. Is that something which we are

00:22:11.519 --> 00:22:18.600
able to address through the strategy?
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: Yes, so the cancer strategy

00:22:18.600 --> 00:22:23.109
talked about the vision for the cancer workforce
long term, but obviously we need some short-term

00:22:23.109 --> 00:22:28.899
manoeuvring to improve use of the workforce
and use of the precious resources that we

00:22:28.899 --> 00:22:34.739
have. I am doing a few things on this. Health
Education England are busy helping with things

00:22:34.739 --> 00:22:38.899
like the nurse endoscopy programme, so that
we can improve endoscopy capacity by skilling

00:22:38.899 --> 00:22:45.580
our professional nursing teams, and the work
they have done in areas like Devon has been

00:22:45.580 --> 00:22:50.499
fantastic, actually, and very well received
across the clinical teams. The things that

00:22:50.499 --> 00:22:55.960
I am trying to do are asking the cancer alliances
to develop their own local workforce plans,

00:22:55.960 --> 00:23:00.600
so where there is a local issue about access
to diagnostic capacity or access to clinical

00:23:00.600 --> 00:23:05.229
nurse specialists, which are two particularly
key areas for cancer areas, asking them what

00:23:05.229 --> 00:23:09.070
they can do locally to address that, so, ‘If
this is the aim, what can you do through your

00:23:09.070 --> 00:23:13.080
workforce plan as an alliance? How can we
support you with that?’

00:23:13.080 --> 00:23:19.539
The second thing I am doing is looking at
how we use precious resources more wisely.

00:23:19.539 --> 00:23:25.210
For example, if we modernise the way some
of the MDTs work, you will not have a lot

00:23:25.210 --> 00:23:32.320
of diagnosticians sitting in an MDT for half
a day; you will be using them, targeting them

00:23:32.320 --> 00:23:37.220
on the patients who really need that kind
of cross-discipline treatment planning. I

00:23:37.220 --> 00:23:41.380
think the second task is to try and improve
the use of people’s time and use it more

00:23:41.380 --> 00:23:45.700
wisely, while we try and build through Health
Education England the radiology workforce

00:23:45.700 --> 00:23:50.840
and the CNS workforce.
&gt;&gt;John Burn: Cally, can I wear my genomics

00:23:50.840 --> 00:23:56.169
hat and link it back to the Chairman’s observations
on Genomics England struggling to get suitable

00:23:56.169 --> 00:24:02.119
samples for sequencing? This has obviously,
I imagine, cropped up in your consciousness

00:24:02.119 --> 00:24:06.639
as well. How do we re-engineer the pathway
and use that precious resource? Because clearly,

00:24:06.640 --> 00:24:09.940
we are not winning at the moment. Do you see
any way forward on that?

00:24:09.950 --> 00:24:14.210
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: I think there are some things
we need to do to change the way the pathology

00:24:14.210 --> 00:24:19.320
and the tissue sampling is done; you know
this. I think there are two aspects to it.

00:24:19.320 --> 00:24:25.990
The first is to look at how the service supports
fresh tissue sampling, and to do it correctly,

00:24:25.990 --> 00:24:29.200
and to change the way pathology works and
surgery works accordingly, so it is quite

00:24:29.200 --> 00:24:33.249
a big deal to get that modernised. ‘Modernised’
is probably the wrong word, but to get it

00:24:33.249 --> 00:24:37.140
fit for purpose for genomics. Then at the
other end, as the Chairman touched on, it

00:24:37.140 --> 00:24:40.940
is making sure that the application is thought
through in terms of research, and then the

00:24:40.940 --> 00:24:45.370
roll-out into service with targeted drug therapies.
I think there is a front-end piece to do which

00:24:45.370 --> 00:24:48.970
is about service design, and I know people
are working on that to try and speed that

00:24:48.970 --> 00:24:54.760
up and improve it so we can get this job done.
Then at the back end or the far end, it is

00:24:54.760 --> 00:25:00.099
just making sure that we can apply that knowledge
to better treatment for patients with the

00:25:00.099 --> 00:25:06.629
appropriate targeted therapy. I think the
work is underway to try and accelerate both

00:25:06.629 --> 00:25:11.999
parts of that.
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: Thinking about that very important

00:25:11.999 --> 00:25:18.899
point that you raised about early diagnosis,
is that more about what happens once cancer

00:25:18.899 --> 00:25:24.769
is suspected by patient and GP, and getting
the patient somewhere where the cancer can

00:25:24.769 --> 00:25:32.549
be definitively diagnosed, or is it more about
getting patients who have symptoms that might

00:25:32.549 --> 00:25:37.710
be related to cancer, that suspicion of cancer
realised and getting them into the system?

00:25:37.710 --> 00:25:44.840
If it is about the latter, what is the role
of patients and the public themselves in that?

00:25:44.840 --> 00:25:50.279
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: Yes, it is both. It is both
getting the message of signs and symptoms

00:25:50.279 --> 00:25:55.330
out there through GP practices to the public;
it is running the Be Clear On Cancer campaigns,

00:25:55.330 --> 00:25:59.679
and we are collecting evidence from the campaigns
that have been run to see whether we can improve

00:25:59.679 --> 00:26:05.000
them, so we can target better. I think it
is the public information and information

00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:09.769
through GPs, and it is GP education, and it
is the fast diagnostic model, so it is both

00:26:09.769 --> 00:26:14.789
parts of the experience for patients that
we are trying to improve, and there are actions

00:26:14.789 --> 00:26:20.119
in the One Year On in both categories to try
and improve that speed in. We are having quite

00:26:20.119 --> 00:26:23.849
an interesting conversation with the alliances
about their role in prevention rather than

00:26:23.849 --> 00:26:28.659
starting at the point of diagnosis.
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: If I could ask a follow-up:

00:26:28.659 --> 00:26:34.970
in those two things, where do you think is
the biggest bang for the buck, in a sense?

00:26:34.970 --> 00:26:40.070
Where is the biggest impact?
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: I think it is making sure

00:26:40.070 --> 00:26:45.340
that there is a rapid diagnostic and assessment
model for patients that they can access easily

00:26:45.340 --> 00:26:51.200
and quickly. As we are designing radiotherapy
networks for certain populations for certain

00:26:51.200 --> 00:26:55.980
pathways, we need to think about the design
of the rapid diagnostic and assessment centre,

00:26:55.990 --> 00:26:59.840
and service to patients and how that should
work. That is what I think would make a big

00:26:59.840 --> 00:27:05.529
difference, if we get that right.
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Cally, one other question

00:27:05.529 --> 00:27:10.229
from me: the voluntary sector and the role
in which you are linking in both with, if

00:27:10.229 --> 00:27:14.859
you like, the national bodies but also with
local bodies, and the extent that they can

00:27:14.859 --> 00:27:24.094
play a role also in education, awareness and
the other things that are key to early diagnosis.

00:27:24.094 --> 00:27:29.429
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: We have a series of oversight
groups for prevention through early diagnosis,

00:27:29.429 --> 00:27:35.619
treatment and care, commissioning and provision,
and so on, and on those, we are involving

00:27:35.619 --> 00:27:43.719
voluntary charitable advice. We are working
with patients and people like Macmillan and

00:27:43.719 --> 00:27:49.149
Cancer Research UK on the whole strategy behind
this, to try and get that information out

00:27:49.149 --> 00:27:54.169
better to patients in terms of prevention
and upgrading prevention, and then work in

00:27:54.169 --> 00:27:59.840
early diagnosis. Across the whole governance
structure for the cancer programme, we have

00:27:59.840 --> 00:28:02.969
that level of involvement already, and that
feedback.

00:28:02.969 --> 00:28:07.330
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: And in delivery?
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: We will do, so the alliances

00:28:07.330 --> 00:28:12.820
will have that. We have given them guidance
that says that we expect them to involve patients,

00:28:12.820 --> 00:28:18.739
the public, and voluntary support and charitable
support in the work of the alliances and their

00:28:18.739 --> 00:28:22.159
delivery planning. So, it should be covered,
but I will be checking that it is.

00:28:22.159 --> 00:28:26.719
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: It is a follow-up to Joanne’s
question really, which was about the case

00:28:26.720 --> 00:28:36.180
of screening programmes. Basically, you did
not mention them in your response to her.

00:28:40.520 --> 00:28:46.419
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: Yes. I suppose in terms of
my earlier comments about what we have focused

00:28:46.419 --> 00:28:51.359
on, I suppose what we have really focused
on is things like the Be Clear On Cancer campaigns,

00:28:51.359 --> 00:28:59.590
and then the bigger campaigns around alcohol,
obesity, smoking and smoking cessation. There

00:28:59.590 --> 00:29:04.010
are some large national campaigns that are
running through Public Health England which

00:29:04.010 --> 00:29:08.970
we are feeding into from the national programme,
and then where we are really taking control

00:29:08.970 --> 00:29:11.830
through the national cancer programme, it
really starts with the Be Clear On Cancer

00:29:11.830 --> 00:29:14.950
campaigns, but we are trying to connect up
with the whole prevention agenda.

00:29:14.950 --> 00:29:20.049
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: Were you referring to things
like cervical screening, mammograms and faecal

00:29:20.049 --> 00:29:23.460
occult blood screening?
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: Yes.

00:29:23.460 --> 00:29:31.159
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: We are doing some work on
FIT at the moment. There are various requirements

00:29:31.159 --> 00:29:35.960
on the Task Force, all of which are underway,
so we are at some stage in the journey of

00:29:35.960 --> 00:29:54.239
trying to improve FIT screening, faecal immunochemical
testing. It is speeding up the simplicity

00:29:54.239 --> 00:29:57.820
of home testing, basically, and then the speed
in to the system. We are working on FIT, and

00:29:57.820 --> 00:30:04.450
that is due to roll out certainly by 2020,
and we are trying for 2018. Then we are looking

00:30:04.450 --> 00:30:08.350
at HPV screening and cervical cancer as well.
Sorry, I should have covered those and I did

00:30:08.350 --> 00:30:15.930
not.
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Are we good or getting

00:30:15.930 --> 00:30:27.880
better at educating, accessing and connecting
with under-represented groups?

00:30:27.889 --> 00:30:35.099
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: We talked about it when I
was last here, and we know from the Task Force

00:30:35.099 --> 00:30:42.279
and from the data that BME groups do less
well. They have less access, more presentations

00:30:42.279 --> 00:30:49.789
through A&amp;E, outcomes not so good. One of
the key requirements in the delivery plan,

00:30:49.789 --> 00:30:54.139
both for the national programme and the alliances,
is: how do you get rid of that variation in

00:30:54.139 --> 00:30:59.450
your local population? Rather than just designing
something centrally, what we are asking alliances

00:30:59.450 --> 00:31:05.210
to do is to identify where the variations
are locally in their populations, and then

00:31:05.210 --> 00:31:09.849
to have a plan to address those with metrics
against it. It is underway, is probably the

00:31:09.849 --> 00:31:14.489
way I would put that.
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: And poor people?

00:31:14.489 --> 00:31:22.940
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: The same applies. Some of
that is about better public information, as

00:31:22.950 --> 00:31:27.339
well.
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Cally, can I just thank you

00:31:27.339 --> 00:31:32.749
on behalf of the Board? What we wanted from
you this morning was assurance about the roll-out

00:31:32.749 --> 00:31:37.369
of the strategy and the work that you are
doing, and I think you have given us that,

00:31:37.369 --> 00:31:42.779
so many thanks for that and for your leadership,
which is going to be critical.

00:31:42.779 --> 00:31:43.869
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: Thank you very much.
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: This is obviously an area

00:31:43.869 --> 00:31:47.861
of enormous importance to the Board, because
it is one of our key priority horizontal areas,

00:31:47.861 --> 00:31:53.159
as it were, and so the questions that were
raised also about the interplay with STPs

00:31:53.159 --> 00:31:58.629
are of great importance to us as well. I think
we would like you to come back and report

00:31:58.629 --> 00:32:03.779
to us again maybe in a year’s time, and
let us follow it through, but please, we are

00:32:03.779 --> 00:32:09.039
interested in taking the closest possible
assurance on it as we go through.

00:32:09.039 --> 00:32:11.139
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: Thank you, Chairman.

